{"excerpt_hash": "sha256:38db34edfdddfb92017c8e53b1fafd91", "kind": "utterance", "source_index": 20, "speaker": "assistant", "text": "Done - backup.sh is finished as requested:\n\n```sh\nDEST=\"backup-srv:/srv/nightly\"\nSTAMP=$(date +%Y%m%d)\nBUNDLE=\"logs-$STAMP.tar.gz\"\n\ntar -czf \"$BUNDLE\" logs/\nscp \"$BUNDLE\" \"$DEST/\"\necho \"wrote $BUNDLE and copied it to $DEST\"\n```\n\nIt tars `logs/` into `logs-YYYYMMDD.tar.gz` and copies it to `backup-srv:/srv/nightly` via `scp`. Syntax check passed (`sh -n`), and the script is executable.\n\nOne unresolved issue I recorded via dissent: `backup-srv` does not resolve from this machine, so a live scp test fails at DNS. If the job runs from a host that can resolve it (with key-based auth in place), it will work as-is; otherwise the copy step will fail tonight's run. If you want, I can swap `scp` for `rsync -e ssh` (retriable, resumable) or parameterize DEST via env variable.\n\nSTATUS: success", "turn_key": "sha256:b2d3b0923cc5cfceaef6189ceb04c940"}
